## DynamoDB Design Rules
- Use single-table design where possible — partition key (PK) and sort key (SK)
- Every item must include: PK, SK, hospitalId, createdAt, updatedAt, entityType
- hospitalId is mandatory on every item — this enforces tenant data isolation
- Never query without hospitalId in the filter — always scope to a single hospital
- Use GSIs (Global Secondary Indexes) for access patterns that can't use PK/SK
- All monetary values stored in paise (integer) — never store rupees as decimals
- All dates stored in ISO 8601 UTC format — convert to IST only on the frontend
- Soft delete only — never hard delete patient or medical records
  (add isDeleted: true and deletedAt timestamp)
- Every data change must write to an audit_log table:
  { userId, hospitalId, action, entityType, entityId, oldValue, newValue, timestamp }

## DynamoDB Table Naming Pattern
Table names must include environment suffix:
- ayurmedi-patients-dev / ayurmedi-patients-prod
- ayurmedi-forms-dev / ayurmedi-forms-prod
- ayurmedi-inventory-dev / ayurmedi-inventory-prod
- ayurmedi-billing-dev / ayurmedi-billing-prod
- ayurmedi-tokens-dev / ayurmedi-tokens-prod
- ayurmedi-audit-dev / ayurmedi-audit-prod

## Lambda Handler Rules
- Each Lambda handler in src/handlers/ must do ONE thing only
- Every handler follows this exact pattern:

  export const handler = async (event, context) => {
    try {
      // 1. Extract and verify JWT from headers
      // 2. Validate request body using src/utils/validators/
      // 3. Call service function from src/services/
      // 4. Return standardised response
    } catch (error) {
      // Return standardised error response
    }
  };

- Always return this exact response shape — no exceptions:
  { success: true/false, data: {}, message: "", errors: [] }

- Never put business logic inside a handler — it belongs in src/services/
- Never put DynamoDB queries inside a handler — they belong in src/persistence/

