## Dynamic Form Engine Rules (Most Critical)
- Every patient form must use the Dynamic Form Engine — never hardcode form fields
- Form templates stored as JSON in DynamoDB forms table
- Each hospital has its own copy of each form template
- Changes to one hospital's form never affect any other hospital
- Form versions are maintained — never overwrite, always create new version
- Form template JSON schema structure:
  {
    "formId": "uuid",
    "hospitalId": "uuid",
    "formType": "REGISTRATION | HISTORY | CLINICAL | PRESCRIPTION | PANCHAKARMA",
    "version": 1,
    "sections": [
      {
        "sectionId": "uuid",
        "title": "Basic Information",
        "fields": [
          {
            "fieldId": "uuid",
            "label": "Patient Name",
            "labelMarathi": "नाव",
            "type": "TEXT | NUMBER | EMAIL | PHONE | DATE | DATETIME | DROPDOWN
                     | MULTI_SELECT | RADIO | CHECKBOX | TEXTAREA | REPEATABLE",
            "required": true,
            "order": 1,
            "validation": { "min": 0, "max": 100, "regex": "" },
            "options": [],
            "defaultValue": ""
          }
        ]
      }
    ]
  }

## API Design Rules
- All API routes follow REST conventions
- Base URL pattern: /api/v1/{resource}
- Validate every request body before processing — use src/utils/validators/
- Never expose internal error details to client in production
- All list endpoints must support: page, limit, search, filters
- hospitalId must always come from verified JWT — never from request body
- Every API call must verify JWT token — no exceptions except /health

## AWS Serverless Rules
- Each Lambda does ONE thing — single responsibility
- Every Lambda must have its own IAM role with least privilege in serverless.yml
- Never hardcode AWS credentials or secrets — use AWS Secrets Manager or env vars
- Lambda timeout: 30s for API, 5 minutes for PDF/report generation
- Lambda memory: 256MB default, 1024MB for PDF generation
- All Lambdas inside VPC to access any private resources
- S3 bucket names: ayurmedi-docs-dev / ayurmedi-docs-prod
- All S3 buckets private — use signed URLs for all file access
- Enable CORS in serverless.yml for frontend domain only

## Code Quality Rules
- Use async/await — never callbacks or raw Promise chains
- Always wrap Lambda handlers in try/catch — never let a Lambda crash silently
- Write a JSDoc comment on every function
- No function longer than 50 lines — break into smaller functions
- No duplicate code — extract to src/utils/ or src/services/
- Validate all environment variables on Lambda cold start
- Never log sensitive data: passwords, tokens, patient health info
- Use structured logging format:
  { level, message, hospitalId, userId, requestId, timestamp }

## Security Rules
- JWT token verified on every single API call via src/middlewares/auth.js
- hospitalId always extracted from JWT — never trusted from request params or body
- Never return passwords, tokens, or secrets in API responses
- Sanitize all user inputs before saving to DynamoDB
- All PHI (patient health info) fields encrypted before storing

